-- title:
--   Finite-memory strategy (finite-state transducer)
-- statement:
--   A strategy $\sigma$ has **finite memory** if it is generated by a finite-state transducer $(\sigma_u,\sigma_a,\mathcal M,m_0)$: $\mathcal M$ is a finite set of memory states, $m_0\in\mathcal M$ the initial memory state, $\sigma_a:\mathcal M\to\mathcal A$ the action selection function and $\sigma_u:\mathcal M\times\mathcal A\times\mathcal S\to\mathcal M$ the memory update function. The transducer generates $\sigma$ by
--
--   $$m_\emptyset=m_0,\qquad m_{h\cdot(a,s)}=\sigma_u(m_h,a,s),\qquad \sigma(h)=\sigma_a(m_h).$$
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper leaves the generation rule implicit; this is the standard one. The memory is an arbitrary finite type `M`, and the update reads the actions recorded in the history, so $\sigma$ is determined on every history.
-- source:
--   Chatterjee, Saona & Ziliotto, Finite-memory strategies in POMDPs with Long-run Average Objectives, arXiv:1904.13360v2, Definition 2.5, p.3

import Mathlib

namespace FiniteMemPOMDP

/-- **Finite-memory strategy.**
Chatterjee, Saona & Ziliotto, arXiv:1904.13360v2, Definition 2.5, p.3: "A strategy σ is said to
have finite memory if it can be modeled by a finite-state transducer. Formally,
σ = (σu, σa, M, m0), where M is a finite set of memory states, m0 is the initial memory state,
σa : M → A is the action selection function and σu : M × A × S → M is the memory update
function."

`IsFiniteMemory σ` holds when there are a finite type `M`, an initial memory `m0 ∈ M`, an
action map `σa : M → A` and an update map `σu : M → A → S → M` such that for every history
`h = ((a_1, s_1), …, (a_n, s_n))`, `σ h = σa(m_h)`, where `m_h` is obtained from `m0` by applying
`σu(·, a_j, s_j)` successively for `j = 1, …, n`.

**Formalization Note** The paper leaves the way a transducer generates `σ` implicit; it is the
standard one, `m_∅ = m0`, `m_{h·(a,s)} = σu(m_h, a, s)`, `σ(h) = σa(m_h)`, written as a left fold
over the chronological history. The memory update reads the actions recorded in the history, so
`σ` is defined on every history, not only on those it generates. -/
def IsFiniteMemory {A S : Type} (σ : List (A × S) → A) : Prop :=
  ∃ (M : Type) (_ : Finite M) (m0 : M) (σa : M → A) (σu : M → A → S → M),
    ∀ h : List (A × S), σ h = σa (h.foldl (fun m x => σu m x.1 x.2) m0)

end FiniteMemPOMDP


